---

<ArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  authorSlug={authorSlug}
  pubDate={pubDate}
  readingTime={readingTime}
  image={image}
  imageAlt={imageAlt}
  headings={headings}
  category="SaaS"
  categorySlug="tools"
  subcategorySlug="saas"
>
  <h2 id="introduction">Digital Note-Taking for Remote Productivity</h2>
  <p>
    Look your note-taking app is basically your second brain. Capturing ideas
    during <a href="/guides/how-to-run-effective-remote-meetings">meetings</a>.
    Organizing research. Building a knowledge base. Managing <a
      href="/tools/best-project-management-software">projects</a
    >. The right tool changes everything about how you work.
  </p>
  <p>
    The note-taking world exploded over the past few years. Notion turned simple
    notes into entire databases. Obsidian and Roam Research brought us networked
    thinking. Evernote rebuilt itself after years of going nowhere. Apple Notes
    became surprisingly powerful when nobody was looking.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spent over sixty hours testing ten different note-taking apps across
    different workflows. Meeting notes, project documentation, personal
    knowledge management, team collaboration. Looked at ease of use,
    organization systems, sync reliability, pricing, and unique features.
  </p>
  <p>
    Short version if you're in a hurry: <a href="/tools/is-notion-worth-it"
      >Notion</a
    > for most remote workers. Obsidian if you're building a personal knowledge base.
    Apple Notes for iPhone and Mac users who want simple and free. Evernote for capturing
    everything. Roam Research for researchers and networked thought.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quick-picks">Quick Picks: Best Note-Taking Apps at a Glance</h2>
  <ComparisonTable
    columns={quickPicksColumns}
    rows={quickPicksRows}
    caption="Quick comparison of top note-taking apps"
  />

  <h2 id="note-taking-approaches">Types of Note-Taking Approaches</h2>
  <p>Before choosing an app understand the different philosophies.</p>

  <h3>Hierarchical / Folder-Based</h3>
  <p>Evernote OneNote Apple Notes Bear.</p>
  <p>
    Traditional organization with notebooks folders and tags. Easy to understand
    works like physical notebooks. Good fit for simple note-taking document
    storage beginners.
  </p>

  <h3>Database / Structured</h3>
  <p>Notion Coda.</p>
  <p>
    Notes as database entries with properties filters and multiple views.
    Powerful for project management and structured information. Makes sense for
    teams project documentation CRM-like note systems.
  </p>

  <h3>Networked / Linked Thinking</h3>
  <p>Obsidian Roam Research Logseq.</p>
  <p>
    Bidirectional links between notes create a second brain knowledge graph.
    Every note can link to any other. Right for researchers writers <a
      href="/guides/productivity">knowledge workers</a
    > building complex ideas.
  </p>

  <h3>AI-Powered / Auto-Linking</h3>
  <p>Mem Reflect.</p>
  <p>
    AI automatically surfaces related notes and creates connections. Less manual
    linking required. Fits people who want the benefits of linked notes without
    manual work.
  </p>

  <h2 id="detailed-reviews">Detailed Reviews: Top 10 Note-Taking Apps</h2>

  <h3 id="notion">Notion . Best All-in-One Workspace</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/notion"
    text="Try Notion Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="notion"
  />

  <p>
    Free for personal use. Plus ten bucks per user monthly. Business fifteen.
    Enterprise custom.<br />Web Windows Mac iOS Android.
  </p>

  <p>What's good</p>

  <p>
    Incredible flexibility. Notes databases wikis task boards calendars in one
    app.<br />Beautiful design. Clean modern interface that makes note-taking
    enjoyable.<br />Generous free tier. Unlimited pages and blocks for personal
    use.<br />Templates galore. Thousands of pre-built templates for every use
    case.<br />Database views. View same data as table kanban calendar gallery
    timeline.<br />Great for teams. Collaboration permissions and commenting
    built-in.<br />Embeds and integrations. Embed Google Docs Figma Miro PDFs
    and more.
  </p>

  <p>What bugs me</p>

  <p>
    Offline mode is limited. Requires internet for full functionality.<br />Can
    be slow. Large workspaces with many blocks load slowly.<br />Steep learning
    curve. So many features it can be overwhelming at first.<br />Markdown
    export issues. Proprietary format makes migration difficult.
  </p>

  <p>
    Makes sense for <a href="/tools/best-team-communication-tools"
      >remote teams</a
    > wanting an all-in-one workspace <a
      href="/tools/best-project-management-software">project managers</a
    > anyone who wants notes plus tasks plus databases in one beautiful app.
  </p>

  <h3 id="obsidian">Obsidian . Best for Linking Ideas</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/obsidian"
    text="Try Obsidian Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="obsidian"
  />

  <p>
    Free for personal use. Sync ten monthly. Publish eight monthly. Commercial
    fifty per user yearly.<br />Windows Mac Linux iOS Android.
  </p>

  <p>What I like</p>

  <p>
    Your notes equal your files. Plain text Markdown files stored locally you
    own everything.<br />Best-in-class linking. Bidirectional links backlinks
    graph view shows connections.<br />Lightning fast. No cloud lag everything
    is local.<br />Extensible. Thousands of community plugins for any workflow.<br
    />Perfect offline. Works without internet since files are local.<br />Free
    forever. No paywalls for core features sync and publish are optional.<br
    />Privacy-focused. Your notes never leave your devices unless you enable
    sync.
  </p>

  <p>What's not great</p>

  <p>
    Sync costs extra. Ten monthly for official sync or use iCloud or Dropbox.<br
    />Steeper learning curve. More technical than Notion or Evernote.<br />Not
    great for teams. Designed for personal knowledge management.<br />No
    databases. Can't replicate Notion's structured data features.
  </p>

  <p>
    Good fit for knowledge workers researchers writers developers anyone
    building a second brain and connecting ideas over time.
  </p>

  <h3 id="roam-research">Roam Research . Best for Networked Thought</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/roam"
    text="Try Roam Research"
    variant="primary"
    program="roam"
  />

  <p>
    Professional fifteen monthly. Believer five hundred for five years.<br />Web
    iOS Android.
  </p>

  <p>You get</p>

  <p>
    Daily notes paradigm. Every day is a page encourages journaling and
    reflection.<br />Bidirectional linking. Pioneered the networked note-taking
    movement.<br />Block-level references.link to specific paragraphs not just
    pages.<br />Cult following.active community of power users and researchers.<br
    />Graph view. Visualize connections between notes.
  </p>

  <p>The tradeoffs</p>

  <p>
    Expensive. Fifteen monthly with no free tier only thirty-one-day trial.<br
    />Dated interface. Looks less polished than Notion or Craft.<br />Buggy at
    times. Occasional sync issues and performance problems.<br />Outshined by
    Obsidian. Obsidian offers similar features for free.
  </p>

  <p>
    Academics researchers and early adopters who bought in before Obsidian or
    Logseq existed. Hard to recommend over Obsidian now unless you're deeply
    committed to Roam's workflow.
  </p>

  <h3 id="evernote">Evernote . Best for Capturing Everything</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/evernote"
    text="Try Evernote Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="evernote"
  />

  <p>
    Free limited. Personal ten eighty-three monthly. Professional fourteen
    seventeen monthly.<br />Web Windows Mac iOS Android.
  </p>

  <p>What works</p>

  <p>
    Web Clipper. Best browser extension for saving articles and research.<br
    />Document scanning. Excellent mobile scanner with OCR text recognition.<br
    />Email to Evernote. Forward emails directly to your notebooks.<br
    />Powerful search. Searches text inside PDFs and images.<br />Reliable sync.
    After years of issues sync is now solid.<br />Tasks integration. Built-in
    task management with reminders.
  </p>

  <p>What doesn't</p>

  <p>
    Free tier is very limited. Sixty MB upload monthly two devices only.<br
    />Expensive. Ten eighty-three monthly is pricey compared to alternatives.<br
    />Bloated. Tries to do everything feels less focused than competitors.<br
    />Lost market share. No longer the default choice it once was.
  </p>

  <p>
    People who clip lots of web articles scan documents frequently or have years
    of notes locked in Evernote.
  </p>

  <h3 id="apple-notes">Apple Notes . Best Free Option Apple Users</h3>

  <p>Free included with iCloud.<br />iOS iPadOS macOS Web at iCloud dot com.</p>

  <p>What's great</p>

  <p>
    Completely free. No premium tiers no upsells.<br />Seamless Apple
    integration. Syncs instantly across iPhone iPad Mac.<br />Fast and simple.
    Opens in milliseconds zero learning curve.<br />Smart Folders. Auto-organize
    notes with tags and filters.<br />Document scanning. Excellent built-in
    scanner on iPhone.<br />Collaboration. Share and co-edit notes with others.<br
    />Locked notes. Password or Face ID protection for sensitive notes.
  </p>

  <p>Downsides</p>

  <p>
    Apple ecosystem only. Terrible on Android or Windows.<br />No advanced
    linking. Can't build knowledge graphs like Obsidian.<br />Limited export. No
    Markdown export hard to migrate away.<br />Basic formatting. Less formatting
    options than competitors.
  </p>

  <p>
    iPhone and Mac users who want simple fast free note-taking without learning
    a complex app. Perfect for casual notes lists and quick capture.
  </p>

  <h3 id="bear">Bear . Best for Apple Users Who Want More</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/bear"
    text="Try Bear Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="bear"
  />

  <p>
    Free iOS and Mac only. Pro one forty-nine monthly or fourteen ninety-nine
    yearly.<br />iOS iPadOS macOS only.
  </p>

  <p>In the package</p>

  <p>
    Beautiful design. Stunning minimal interface with gorgeous themes.<br
    />Markdown native. Write in clean Markdown perfect for writers.<br />Fast
    and lightweight. Feels snappier than Apple Notes.<br />Tag-based
    organization. Nested tags like work slash projects.<br />Export anywhere.
    Export to PDF DOCX HTML Markdown.<br />Affordable. One forty-nine monthly is
    one of the cheapest premium note apps.
  </p>

  <p>What's annoying</p>

  <p>
    Apple only. No Windows Android or web version.<br />Sync requires Pro. Free
    tier doesn't sync across devices.<br />No bidirectional links. Can't build
    knowledge graphs.<br />Limited collaboration. Not designed for teams.
  </p>

  <p>
    Apple users who want a beautiful Markdown-focused writing experience and are
    willing to pay one forty-nine monthly for sync.
  </p>

  <h3 id="craft">Craft . Best for Beautiful Notes</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/craft"
    text="Try Craft Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="craft"
  />

  <p>
    Free limited. Pro five monthly. Business ten per user monthly.<br />iOS
    iPadOS macOS Windows Web.
  </p>

  <p>What's in there</p>

  <p>
    Stunning design. Most beautiful note-taking app hands down.<br />Daily
    notes. Roam-style daily notes with linking.<br />Share as web pages. Publish
    notes as beautiful public pages.<br />Markdown support. Write in Markdown
    with live preview.<br />Great free tier. One thousand blocks daily is
    generous.
  </p>

  <p>What's not</p>

  <p>
    Expensive. Five monthly for features others offer free.<br />Limited export.
    Hard to migrate notes out.<br />Not as powerful as Notion. Lacks databases
    and advanced features.<br />Small user base. Smaller community than Notion
    or Obsidian.
  </p>

  <p>
    Creatives designers and anyone who values aesthetics. Great for
    documentation and sharing beautiful notes publicly.
  </p>

  <h3 id="logseq">Logseq . Best Open-Source Alternative</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/logseq"
    text="Try Logseq Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="logseq"
  />

  <p>Free open source.<br />Windows Mac Linux iOS Android.</p>

  <p>You get</p>

  <p>
    Completely free and open-source. No paywalls community-driven.<br
    />Outliner-based. Every note is an outline great for structured thinking.<br
    />Bidirectional linking. Roam-style networked notes.<br />Local-first like
    Obsidian. Your files stored locally as Markdown.<br />Graph view. Visualize
    connections between notes.<br />Plugin ecosystem. Extensible with community
    plugins.
  </p>

  <p>What could be better</p>

  <p>
    Less polished. Rougher edges than commercial apps.<br />Sync is manual. Use
    Git iCloud or Dropbox for sync.<br />Steeper learning curve. Outliner
    paradigm takes getting used to.
  </p>

  <p>
    Privacy advocates open-source enthusiasts people who want Roam-style notes
    without the fifteen monthly cost.
  </p>

  <h3 id="mem">Mem . Best AI-Powered Notes</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/mem"
    text="Try Mem"
    variant="primary"
    program="mem"
  />

  <p>Fifteen monthly no free tier.<br />Web iOS Android.</p>

  <p>Features</p>

  <p>
    AI-powered connections. Automatically surfaces related notes.<br />Smart
    Search. Natural language queries find relevant notes.<br />No folders. Just
    dump notes AI organizes them.<br />Email to Mem. Forward emails and they
    become notes.
  </p>

  <p>Downsides</p>

  <p>
    Expensive. Fifteen monthly with no free tier or trial.<br />Requires
    internet. Cloud-only no offline mode.<br />Less control. AI organization
    means less manual structure.<br />Small feature set. Simpler than Notion or
    Obsidian.
  </p>

  <p>
    Early adopters who want AI to do the heavy lifting people who hate manual
    organization.
  </p>

  <h3 id="onenote">OneNote . Best Free Microsoft Alternative</h3>
  <AffiliateButton
    href="/go/onenote"
    text="Try OneNote Free"
    variant="primary"
    program="onenote"
  />

  <p>
    Free includes five GB OneDrive storage.<br />Windows Mac iOS Android Web.
  </p>

  <p>What works</p>

  <p>
    Completely free. No premium tiers or paywalls.<br />Infinite canvas. Place
    notes anywhere on the page not just top-to-bottom.<br />Great for stylus
    users. Excellent handwriting and drawing on iPad or Surface.<br />Microsoft
    integration. Works with Office Teams Outlook.<br />OCR in images. Search for
    text inside handwritten notes.
  </p>

  <p>What's not great</p>

  <p>
    Dated interface. Feels old compared to modern apps.<br />Sync can be slow.
    OneDrive sync is less reliable than competitors.<br />No Markdown. Can't
    write in plain text.<br />Confusing organization. Notebooks sections pages
    can be hard to manage.
  </p>

  <p>
    Windows users Microsoft three sixty-five subscribers students who need free
    note-taking with handwriting support.
  </p>

  <h2 id="feature-comparison">Feature Comparison Matrix</h2>
  <ComparisonTable
    columns={featureComparisonColumns}
    rows={featureComparisonRows}
    caption="Feature comparison across note-taking apps"
